# Milestone Status and Progress (every PR)

A milestone's state is part of the deliverable, not an afterthought at release time. **The pull
request that closes an issue also moves the milestone**, so the documents are never staler than the
work they describe.

## What every pull request does

1. **Update the docs the change touches.** A new setting means `.env.example` (`make env-example`);
   a new permission means the RBAC matrix (`make rbac-matrix`); a new command, endpoint or workflow
   means the README or the document that already describes that area. A reviewer should never learn
   something from the diff that the docs still contradict.
2. **Update the milestone's progress:**

   ```bash
   make milestone-progress
   ```

   It reads the issue states from GitHub (issues only — pull requests are not counted) and writes,
   into the milestone document's header table, the README's *Delivery at a glance* table and the
   *Milestone summary* table in `docs/GITHUB/README.md` (whose **Status** column it owns, with a
   roll-up line under it):

   ```text
   | **Progress** | 🟩🟩🟩🟩🟩⬜⬜⬜⬜⬜ **50%** (4/8 issues) |
   ```

   Ten cells, green for done, with the percentage and the count behind it. Commit the result in the
   same pull request.
3. **Check it before pushing:** `make milestone-progress-check` fails when a document disagrees with
   GitHub and prints which one.

### When the pull requests are stacked

A stacked branch cannot do step 2 honestly: its issue is still open, so the generator would write a
bar that is about to be wrong. In that case the bar moves in the PR that **merges last**, or in a
short follow-up PR opened on the same branch afterwards — never by hand-editing it forward.

A follow-up PR of that kind is still an issue branch: it keeps the `Issue/<N>/<slug>` branch and the
`Issue <N>: ` commit prefix, and its description says **`Refs #<N>`**, not `Closes #<N>`, because the
issue is already closed. Name its description file `PR_<N>_FOLLOWUP_DESCRIPTION.md`.

Two things to know when the milestone is already closed on GitHub: `gh pr create --milestone` cannot
find a **closed** milestone and fails the whole command, so create the PR first and attach it after —

```bash
num=$(gh api "repos/<owner>/<repo>/milestones?state=all" --jq '.[] | select(.title | startswith("Milestone <n>")) | .number')
gh api -X PATCH "repos/<owner>/<repo>/issues/<pr>" -F milestone="$num"
```

## When the last issue of a milestone closes

The pull request that closes it **also marks the milestone done**, in three places:

- the milestone document's **Status** row: `✅ Done: issues <first>–<last> closed on <date>, release
  note [`v0.<n>.0`](../RELEASES/RELEASE_v0_<n>_0.md)`;
- its **Exit criteria**: tick what is met, and for anything met only in part, say **which half is
  done and what the other half waits for** — never tick a criterion that is not true;
- the **GitHub milestone** itself: `make milestone-progress ARGS='--close-completed'`.

Then write the release note (`docs/GITHUB/RELEASES/RELEASE_v0_<n>_0.md`) from the template in
`docs/GITHUB/RELEASES/README.md`, and only then is the tag cut.

## Sprints

The sprint tables in [`docs/TEAM/WORKLOAD_SPLIT.md` §3](../../docs/TEAM/WORKLOAD_SPLIT.md) are
**marked by hand**, because a lane names issues it works *against* — a contract stub, a fixture, a
forward reference such as `[→95]` — as well as the ones it delivers, so no script can tell the two
apart. A sprint has **three** states in *Where we are*, and a sprint spanning two milestones will
sit in the middle one for a while:

| State | When | What the row says after the marker |
|---|---|---|
| 📋 planned / 📋 next | nothing delivered yet | nothing |
| 🚧 in progress | **some** of its delivered issues are closed | what closed; what has not started |
| ✅ done | **every** issue its lanes deliver is closed | which milestone closed, and its tag |

**A sprint that carries two milestones is not done when the first one closes.** Sprint 5 delivers M4
and M5: when M4 closed and M5 had not started, the row became `🚧 in progress` and the
"**N of 14 sprints done**" line stayed put, with one sentence under the table saying *why* it had
not moved. Leaving it on `📋 next` would have been wrong, and ticking it would have been worse.

The pull request that moves a sprint into either later state:

- updates its row in *Where we are* and, if it finished the sprint, the "**N of 14 sprints done**"
  line and the `📋 next` marker on the sprint after it;
- puts `✅` in front of that sprint's number in *The lanes* table once it is done;
- updates the root `README.md` **Status** block (below).

All fourteen sprints run in **semester 2**. Say `semester 2` where a document names one; there is no
semester 1 in this schedule.

## The hand-written status nobody generates

`make milestone-progress` owns the **bars** and nothing else. These live beside them, say the same
thing in prose, and go stale silently — check all three whenever a milestone or a sprint moves:

1. **The root `README.md` `## Status` block.** Its phase sentence (sprints done, issues closed,
   latest tag, what is next) **and** its milestone checklist. Its issue count must equal the
   generated *Delivery at a glance* total a few lines above it — they have disagreed before, and the
   hand-written one was the wrong one. A milestone whose tag is not cut yet is ticked as
   ``(M<n>, `v0.<n>.0` to cut)``, never with a bare tag it does not have.
2. **The delivery gantt in
   [`docs/PLAN/IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md`](../../docs/PLAN/IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md).** A shipped
   milestone gets `done`, combined with `crit` where that is already set
   (`M3 Identity & RBAC  :crit, done, m3, 2, 2`). It went three milestones without being touched.
3. **Any note explaining a temporary disagreement between two rows.** Such a note is written for a
   state that ends, and **the pull request that ends it deletes the note** — a paragraph explaining
   why a bar reads 12% is worse than useless once the bar reads 100%, because a reader goes looking
   for a number that is not on the page.

## Rules

- **Progress is read from GitHub, never typed by hand.** A hand-edited bar is a claim; a generated
  one is a fact. If the number looks wrong, the issue states are wrong — fix those.
- **A bar at 100% and an open issue cannot coexist.** The script counts issues in the milestone, so
  an issue closed without its work done inflates it; close issues when the work merges, not before.
- **Partly-met exit criteria stay unticked, with the reason written next to them.** "Partly (Issue
  N): <what is done> — <what it waits for>" is the shape; see M2 and M3 for worked examples.
- The bar is emoji, not an image: it renders on GitHub, in an editor and in a terminal, with no
  external image host and no network.
- **Never tick something ahead of the fact it claims.** A milestone is not done before its last
  issue closes, a sprint is not done before every issue it delivers closes, and a tag is not
  referenced before it is cut. Each of those is cheap to state honestly and expensive to unpick.

## Before pushing

```bash
make milestone-progress-check     # the bars agree with GitHub
```

Then read your own diff against this list: the milestone header, the sprint row, the README Status
block and its checklist, the gantt, and any note whose subject has gone away. The generator catches
exactly one of those five.
